<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>dispersion</title></head><body><h1>dispersion</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Dispersive Power</li><li>Dispersive Power</li><li>Pure Spectrum and Dispersive Power</li><li>Chromatic and Spherical Aberration</li><li>Chromatic and Spherical Aberration</li><li>Achromatism and Its Applications</li><li>Achromatism</li></ul><h2>Dispersive Power</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Dispersion:** splitting of white light into its constituent colours because the refractive index of a material depends on wavelength. This occurs because the speed of light in a medium varies with wavelength — violet light (shorter wavelength) slows down more than red light (longer wavelength), so it bends more.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Pure spectrum:** a spectrum in which colours do not overlap. Achieved with a narrow slit and a single prism so that each wavelength emerges at a distinct angle. The narrow slit ensures each colour comes from a single point source, eliminating colour blending.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Dispersive power $\omega$:** $\omega = \frac{n_v - n_r}{n_y - 1}$, where $n_v$, $n_r$, $n_y$ are refractive indices for violet, red, and yellow (sodium D line). Dispersive power is a dimensionless quantity — it is the ratio of angular dispersion to the mean deviation.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Crown vs flint glass:** crown glass has $\omega \approx 0.016$, flint glass has $\omega \approx 0.031$. Flint glass disperses about twice as much, which is why it is used in spectroscope prisms where a wide angular spread is desired.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Angular dispersion:** $\delta_v - \delta_r = (n_v - n_r)A$ for a small-angle prism, or equivalently $(n_v - n_r)A = \omega(n_y - 1)A$. The spread of colours is proportional to both the refractive-index difference and the prism angle $A$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Deviation:** for a small-angle prism, the mean deviation is $\delta_y = (n_y - 1)A$. The prism bends all wavelengths, but different amounts — this differential bending is what creates the spectrum.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Why dispersion occurs:** the refractive index follows Cauchy's relation $n(\lambda) = A + \frac{B}{\lambda^2} + \frac{C}{\lambda^4}$, so shorter wavelengths have higher $n$ and bend more. This wavelength dependence is a property of the electronic response of the material.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Unit analysis:** dispersive power is dimensionless (ratio of two quantities with the same units). Angular dispersion has units of radians. The prism angle $A$ is in radians or degrees.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Rainbow formation:** a natural example of dispersion — sunlight undergoes refraction, internal reflection, and a second refraction inside water droplets, separating into the seven colours of the spectrum.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Dispersive power is dimensionless and material-only: it compares how much a material separates colours relative to how much it bends them.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Dispersive power is NOT the same as angular dispersion — dispersive power is dimensionless (a ratio), while angular dispersion has units of angle and depends on the prism angle.</td></tr><tr><td>A larger refractive index does not necessarily mean a larger dispersive power — flint glass has both a higher $n$ and a higher $\omega$ than crown glass, but the two properties are independent.</td></tr><tr><td>Dispersion is NOT caused by the prism absorbing different colours — all colours pass through; they simply bend by different amounts because $n$ depends on wavelength.</td></tr><tr><td>The colours in a spectrum do not have sharp boundaries — the seven colours (VIBGYOR) are a convention. The spectrum is a continuous gradient of wavelengths.</td></tr><tr><td>For a large-angle prism, the small-angle approximation $\delta = (n-1)A$ is no longer valid — one must use Snell's law at each surface separately.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Pure spectrum and dispersive power is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Dispersive Power</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>White light contains a range of wavelengths. In ordinary glass, refractive index is greater for violet than red, so violet bends more through a prism: this wavelength-dependent separation is dispersion.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For a thin prism of apex angle A and small deviations, a colour with refractive index n has deviation δ=(n−1)A. Therefore the red–violet angular spread is Δδ=(nv−nr)A.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Dispersive power compares colour spread with mean yellow deviation: ω=(δv−δr)/δy=(nv−nr)/(ny−1). It is dimensionless and describes relative dispersion of the material, not the prism angle.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Worked example: nv=1.532, nr=1.514 and ny=1.523 give ω=0.018/0.523≈0.0344. A higher ω means more red–violet separation for the same mean deviation.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The thin-prism ratio follows by dividing Δδ=(nv−nr)A by δy=(ny−1)A; A cancels. In a different surrounding medium, use refractive indices relative to that medium.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>For normal dispersion in glass, violet deviates more than red.</td></tr><tr><td>Dispersive power is a relative spread; total deviation also depends on A.</td></tr><tr><td>The thin-prism formula assumes small angles.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not confuse dispersive power with total deviation; total deviation depends on apex angle.</td></tr><tr><td>Use small-angle thin-prism relations only within their approximation.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A prism separates colours because refractive index varies with wavelength. For a thin prism, ω=(nv−nr)/(ny−1) measures relative colour spread.</p><hr><h2>Pure Spectrum and Dispersive Power</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A prism forms a spectrum because refractive index varies with wavelength. In normal dispersion, violet has a larger index and deviation than red. A pure spectrum has narrow, well-separated colour images with little overlap.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For a thin prism, angular dispersion is Δδ=(nv−nr)A and yellow mean deviation is δy=(ny−1)A. Their ratio is ω=Δδ/δy=(nv−nr)/(ny−1).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Worked example: if δy=3° and ω=0.06, the red–violet spread is Δδ=0.18°. A material with twice the dispersive power gives twice the spread at the same mean deviation.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>To obtain a purer spectrum, use a narrow slit, collimate the incident beam and focus the dispersed rays with a lens. A wide slit broadens each colour image and causes neighbouring bands to overlap.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>A spectrometer uses a collimator slit and telescope to form and inspect a sharp spectrum. Resolution improves when the source image is narrow and the optical alignment is correct.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>A pure spectrum minimises overlap of adjacent colour images.</td></tr><tr><td>Dispersive power is relative spread, not absolute deviation.</td></tr><tr><td>Narrow slits improve resolution at the cost of brightness.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not equate a broad rainbow with a pure spectrum; slit width affects purity.</td></tr><tr><td>The thin-prism ratio assumes small deviations.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A pure spectrum requires narrow, collimated light and well-separated colour images. Thin-prism dispersive power is ω=(nv−nr)/(ny−1).</p><hr><h2>Chromatic and Spherical Aberration</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Chromatic aberration is colour fringing caused by a lens having different focal lengths for different wavelengths. In ordinary glass, blue/violet light is refracted more strongly than red, so their images do not share one focus.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Longitudinal chromatic aberration is the axial separation Δf=|fv−fr| of two colour foci. Transverse chromatic aberration is a wavelength-dependent lateral image displacement, often visible at the edge of a wide field.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Spherical aberration has a different cause: marginal and paraxial rays of the same colour focus at different points because the surface is spherical. It can be reduced with an aperture stop or aspheric surfaces.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Worked comparison: if red focuses at 50.0 cm and blue at 49.0 cm, longitudinal chromatic aberration is 1.0 cm. This is axial focus separation, not a 1 cm sideways image shift.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>An achromatic doublet pairs converging crown glass with diverging flint glass so two selected colours focus together. Stopping down reduces marginal-ray error but does not remove material dispersion.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Chromatic error depends on wavelength; spherical error depends on ray height.</td></tr><tr><td>Achromats correct two selected colours; aperture stops reject marginal rays.</td></tr><tr><td>Aperture reduction also reduces image brightness.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Stopping down does not make glass non-dispersive.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary achromatism reduces selected colour separation but may leave secondary spectrum.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Chromatic aberration comes from wavelength-dependent focal length; spherical aberration comes from paraxial–marginal focus separation. Their corrections differ.</p><hr><h2>Chromatic and Spherical Aberration</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Chromatic aberration is colour fringing caused by a lens having different focal lengths for different wavelengths. In ordinary glass, blue/violet light is refracted more strongly than red, so their images do not share one focus.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Longitudinal chromatic aberration is the axial separation Δf=|fv−fr| of two colour foci. Transverse chromatic aberration is a wavelength-dependent lateral image displacement, often visible at the edge of a wide field.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Spherical aberration has a different cause: marginal and paraxial rays of the same colour focus at different points because the surface is spherical. It can be reduced with an aperture stop or aspheric surfaces.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Worked comparison: if red focuses at 50.0 cm and blue at 49.0 cm, longitudinal chromatic aberration is 1.0 cm. This is axial focus separation, not a 1 cm sideways image shift.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>An achromatic doublet pairs converging crown glass with diverging flint glass so two selected colours focus together. Stopping down reduces marginal-ray error but does not remove material dispersion.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Chromatic error depends on wavelength; spherical error depends on ray height.</td></tr><tr><td>Achromats correct two selected colours; aperture stops reject marginal rays.</td></tr><tr><td>Aperture reduction also reduces image brightness.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Stopping down does not make glass non-dispersive.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary achromatism reduces selected colour separation but may leave secondary spectrum.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Chromatic aberration comes from wavelength-dependent focal length; spherical aberration comes from paraxial–marginal focus separation. Their corrections differ.</p><hr><h2>Achromatism and Its Applications</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>An optical pair is achromatic for two chosen colours when their wavelength-dependent deviations cancel and both colours share a final focus. The lenses remain dispersive individually; their effects compensate.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For two thin lenses in contact with signed focal lengths f1,f2 and dispersive powers ω1,ω2, primary achromatism requires ω1/f1+ω2/f2=0. A crown converging lens is commonly paired with a more dispersive flint diverging lens.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Worked design: f1=+20 cm, ω1=0.02 and ω2=0.04 require 0.02/20+0.04/f2=0, giving f2=−40 cm. The negative sign identifies a diverging flint element.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The net power is 1/F=1/f1+1/f2, so the doublet can remain converging while cancelling colour spread. Camera, telescope and microscope objectives use achromatic groups to reduce coloured fringes.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Primary achromatism corrects two selected wavelengths; other colours may still form a secondary spectrum. More elaborate apochromatic systems correct additional wavelengths.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Opposite-signed powers let dispersion cancel while net power remains nonzero.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep focal-length signs in the achromatism equation.</td></tr><tr><td>Two-colour correction can leave secondary spectrum.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not omit the negative focal length of the diverging lens.</td></tr><tr><td>The simple condition assumes thin lenses in contact.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> An achromatic doublet balances wavelength dispersion with signed lens powers: ω1/f1+ω2/f2=0. It reduces but does not eliminate every colour error.</p><hr><h2>Achromatism</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>An optical pair is achromatic for two chosen colours when their wavelength-dependent deviations cancel and both colours share a final focus. The lenses remain dispersive individually; their effects compensate.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For two thin lenses in contact with signed focal lengths f1,f2 and dispersive powers ω1,ω2, primary achromatism requires ω1/f1+ω2/f2=0. A crown converging lens is commonly paired with a more dispersive flint diverging lens.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Worked design: f1=+20 cm, ω1=0.02 and ω2=0.04 require 0.02/20+0.04/f2=0, giving f2=−40 cm. The negative sign identifies a diverging flint element.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The net power is 1/F=1/f1+1/f2, so the doublet can remain converging while cancelling colour spread. Camera, telescope and microscope objectives use achromatic groups to reduce coloured fringes.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Primary achromatism corrects two selected wavelengths; other colours may still form a secondary spectrum. More elaborate apochromatic systems correct additional wavelengths.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Opposite-signed powers let dispersion cancel while net power remains nonzero.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep focal-length signs in the achromatism equation.</td></tr><tr><td>Two-colour correction can leave secondary spectrum.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not omit the negative focal length of the diverging lens.</td></tr><tr><td>The simple condition assumes thin lenses in contact.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> An achromatic doublet balances wavelength dispersion with signed lens powers: ω1/f1+ω2/f2=0. It reduces but does not eliminate every colour error.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Dispersive Power</td><td>Pure spectrum and dispersive power is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Dispersive Power</td><td>A prism separates colours because refractive index varies with wavelength. For a thin prism, ω=(nv−nr)/(ny−1) measures relative colour spread.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Pure Spectrum and Dispersive Power</td><td>A pure spectrum requires narrow, collimated light and well-separated colour images. Thin-prism dispersive power is ω=(nv−nr)/(ny−1).</td></tr><tr><td>4: Chromatic and Spherical Aberration</td><td>Chromatic aberration comes from wavelength-dependent focal length; spherical aberration comes from paraxial–marginal focus separation. Their corrections differ.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Chromatic and Spherical Aberration</td><td>Chromatic aberration comes from wavelength-dependent focal length; spherical aberration comes from paraxial–marginal focus separation. Their corrections differ.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Achromatism and Its Applications</td><td>An achromatic doublet balances wavelength dispersion with signed lens powers: ω1/f1+ω2/f2=0. It reduces but does not eliminate every colour error.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Achromatism</td><td>An achromatic doublet balances wavelength dispersion with signed lens powers: ω1/f1+ω2/f2=0. It reduces but does not eliminate every colour error.</td></tr></table></body></html>